/*
	LESSON 02 - SYNTHDEFS AND ENVGENS
Before we proceed, we need to explore SynthDefs, EnvGens and doneActions in sufficient detail.
I currently don't have time to give a detailed overview so reflect on the following...
- a Synthdef specifies how to create a UGen graph (a graph of DSP blocks)
- The resulting UGen graph from a synthdef is "static". This means it cannot be modified dynamically after creation. Parameters can be changed, but one cannot add new behaviour on the fly that was not possible when the synthdef was defined. You define it once and change its behaviour through parameters. One way I like to think about it, is that the UGen graph of a synthdef has no internal state. Idk if this is correct, but it's the view I consider for now.
This is especially important for Synthdefs that rely on randomness.

- TIP: To access documentation in the IDE, type the name and hit Ctrl+D on the line for documentation. D for documentation.
*/




/*
SYNTHS
A synth is the client-side representation of a synth node on the server.
Synth nodes are defined using SynthDefs, hence you should expect to see the keyword `SynthDef` prior to the keyword `Synth`.
Buses are an important concept used to specify the interconnection between synth nodes. More on these later.
For now, all we care about Synth is how it is often used.
After you create a SynthDef named \piano, you access it as follows:
- Synth(\piano) // with optional list of args and values.

SYNTHDEFS IN A NUTSHELL
A SynthDef can be viewed as a recipe for making or processing audio.
How? It specifies how various UGens are connected - how signals flow, etc. This gives us something called a UGen graph.

General syntax for creating synthdefs:
x = SynthDef(\name, uGenGraphfunction);
x.add; // adds it to the server. See also x.play, etc.
We can also do x.play,

SYNTHS THAT FREE THEMSELVES
Env
- An Env describes a segmented envelope. It is what you feed to an EnvGen, which is a UGen specialized for envelopes.
- An envelope is conceptualized as a sequence of tuples. Each tuple (aka node) has three parameters: target level, time duration from previous tuple, and a shape. Well...sorta. In practice these are specified separately.

- Env.new(levels: [ 0, 1, 0 ], // start from 0, go to 1, go to 0
          times: [ 1, 1 ], // take 1 second to ramp (0,1) and take 1 second to ramp down (1, 0)
          curve: 'lin', // suggests the shape is a ramp
)
Note: levels can themselves be generated by any UGen if the envelope is used with an EnvGen. Multichannel level expansion is possible when the array itself contains arrays.
Curve: can be an array of symbols. Values include
	\step
    \hold, \lin, \exp, \sin, \sqr, \cub, etc.


EnvGen.kr(envelope,
          gate: 1.0,
          levelScale: 1.0,
          levelBias: 0.0,
          timeScale: 1.0,
          doneAction: 0
)
Of the arguments, the most important are envelope and doneAction.
Envelope is just an instance of Env. How EnvGen uses Env - when the EnvGen is triggered, it polls the envelope object. The Env input can be other UGens.
The 'gate' argument is...I think it's used to trigger the EnvGen. The doc says "If gate < 0, force a release with time -1.0". Force release means cut off the envelope immediately. But the amount of time for it to cutoff is the amount less than -1. For example, -2.0 is a 1-second cutoff.
doneAction: an integer. Setting this equal to 2 will free the synth. But which synth?

Done
Some UGens set a "done" flag once they are done playing.
The Done object can be used to explicitly track this flag for some UGen object. The list of UGens trackable by Done include:
- PlayBuf, RecordBuf, Line, XLine, EnvGen, BufRd, BufWr, etc.
doneActions
Some UGens have this as a parameter. It indicates a subroutine to execute once the done flag is set. You can access by name
(0, none), (1, pauseSelf), (2, freeSelf). 2 is the most important for now. Later we'll see freeGroup, freeAllInGroup, etc.
So far, it looks like deon-ness is contagious. When a synth is done, it seems its partners are also forced to be done.

HOW ENVGENS ARE USED: Just multiply by the signal. The envelope is supposed to control the amplitude, remember?

Enough chit-chat!
*/

// Boot the server
s.boot;


// Define the SynthDef without the envelope
(
SynthDef(\naiveSine,
	{|freq = 440.0, amp = 0.6| // shorthand for arg
		var sig;
		sig = SinOsc.ar(freq: freq, mul: amp);
		// remember, every SynthDef must write its output somewhere
		Out.ar(0, sig);
	}
).add;
)


// instantiate and play sound.
/*
(
var n=6, baseFreq=220.0;
var amplitude = 1/n;
postln(n); postln(amplitude);
n.do(
	Synth(\naiveSine, [\freq, baseFreq*n, \amp, 1/n]);
);
)
*/
Synth(\naiveSine, [\freq, 410.01, \amp, 0.25]);
Synth(\naiveSine, [\freq, 420.4, \amp, 0.25]);
Synth(\naiveSine, [\freq, 440.0, \amp, 0.25]);
Synth(\naiveSine, [\freq, 500.30, \amp, 0.25]);

s.freeAll;


// Define a version of the SynthDef with envelope.
(
SynthDef(\envSine,
	{ |freq=440.0, amp=0.6, tup=0.1, tdn=0.4|
		var env, sig;
		env = EnvGen.kr(
			Env([0, amp, 0],
				[tup, tdn],
				'lin'
			),
			doneAction: 0 // we still have to free manually.
		);
		sig = SinOsc.ar(freq: freq, mul: amp);
		sig = env * sig;
		Out.ar(0, sig);
	}
).add;
)

// instantiate
Synth(\envSine, [\freq, 68.midicps, \tup, 0.01, \tdn, 0.3]);
Synth(\envSine, [\freq, 70.midicps, \tup, 0.01, \tdn, 0.6]);
Synth(\envSine, [\freq, 72.midicps, \tup, 0.01, \tdn, 0.9]);
Synth(\envSine, [\freq, 73.midicps, \tup, 0.01, \tdn, 1.2]);
Synth(\envSine, [\freq, 75.midicps, \tup, 0.01, \tdn, 1.5]);
Synth(\envSine, [\freq, 77.midicps, \tup, 0.01, \tdn, 3.0]);
Synth(\envSine, [\freq, 79.midicps, \tup, 0.01, \tdn, 4.0]);
Synth(\envSine, [\freq, 80.midicps, \tup, 0.01, \tdn, 6.0]);

s.freeAll;


// Now, define a version of the SynthDef with a proper doneAction
// All that changes is doneAction set to 2.
// We also make cosmetic changes to the synth
(
SynthDef(\doneSine,
	{ |freq=440.0, amp=0.6, dur=1.0|
		var env, sig;
		var tup = 0.1*dur, tdn = 0.5*dur;
		env = EnvGen.kr(
			Env([0, amp, amp/2, 0],
				[tup, dur-(tdn+tup), tdn],
				'lin'
			),
			doneAction: 2
		);
		sig = SinOsc.ar(freq: freq, mul: amp);
		sig = env * sig;
		Out.ar(0, sig);
	}
).add;
)

// instantiate. No need to free!
Synth(\doneSine, [\freq, 68.midicps, \dur, 0.5]);
Synth(\doneSine, [\freq, 70.midicps, \dur, 0.3]);
Synth(\doneSine, [\freq, 72.midicps, \dur, 1.0]);
Synth(\doneSine, [\freq, 73.midicps, \dur, 2.01]);
Synth(\doneSine, [\freq, 75.midicps, \dur, 3.01]);
Synth(\doneSine, [\freq, 77.midicps, \dur, 4.01]);
Synth(\doneSine, [\freq, 79.midicps, \dur, 5.01]);
Synth(\doneSine, [\freq, 80.midicps, \dur, 6.01]);


s.freeAll; // just for straddlers


/*
FOR THE NEXT SEVERAL LESSONS
We will continue to use this very basic synth with its simple timbre and primitive envelope.
We will instead spend much of our time focusing on patterns then samples and audio buffers.
Once comfortable with that we can then spend effort on proper audio synthesis, effects processing, etc.
The reason is simple - we are treating SuperCollider as a musical instrument.
When a beginner pianist begins with piano we don't emphasize complex articulation, legato, etc. We simply teach him how to play notes, chords and scales as well as singing along to them.
Once he is comfortable with that he may then begin flavoring his playing using dynamic techniques.
*/



/*
LOTSA DETAIL
(but not too much)
- To understand SC properly we have to understand the Server architecture.
- The server is a synthesis engine.
- The synthesis engine has a decent amount of runtime dynamism; patches can be created and destroyed at runtime, sample buffers can be created, allocated, reallocated, etc.
- We can also patch between modules using control buses and global audio.

CLIENT-SIDE vs SERVER-SIDE
SuperCollider consists of a bundled client and server architecture.
The client should be the interpreted sclang, while the server would be the scsynth aduio server.
The client and server are different worlds. The same thing looks different on the client compared to the server. That kinda stuff.
Client side stuff is usually called "objects".
Server side stuff is usually called "nodes".

- Commands are all received via TCP or UDP based on the OSC protocol.
- The server does not send or receive MIDI; it is the responsibility of the client to receive MIDI and convert it to necessary control signals for the server, or to receive signals from the server and convert to MIDI.

NODES
- The synth engine often works with a data strucuture described as a tree of nodes. What's a node? A node is a generalization of two concepts - a Synth and a Group.

- Keep this in mind: every node has an integer id.

- Group: a collection of nodes represented as a linked list.
The idea is that they can all be controlled together. The nodes in a group

- Synth: a collection of UGens that run together.
We control them by providing commands to the synthesis engine.
They can read input from and write output to control buses and global audio.
Synths are created from SynthDefs.

SYNTHDEFS
SynthDefs exist as files loaded into the synth server.
They are often referred to by name.
A SYNTHDEF CREATES A SYNTH!!! Keep this in mind. Once you have run a SynthDef, the synth node exists on the audio server.
By default, you can make at most 1024 synth definitions.
In practice, you will never reach that amount.

AUDIO BUSES
There is a global array of audio buses which synths use to send audio signals to each other.
The array is zero-indexed and, iirc, you can specify the max number of buses.
Buses may be viewed as a generic way for allowing synths to communicate - generally, different synths may have different interfaces. Instead of having to know each other's interface the synths can just write to a global bus and read from it.
The lowest-numbered buses write to audio output.

CONTROL BUSES
There's also a global array of control buses, which synths can use to send control signals to each other.

BUFFERS
A Buffer in SC is an array of 32-bit floating point values with a small descriptive header.
They are all stored in a global zero-indexed array.
Can be used for wavetables, sample buffers, delay lines, envelopes, etc. Envelopes? That's interesting.
They don't even necessarily need to be audio - a buffer is just an array of floating point values which may be processed at audio rate.



*/


